Hi Pierre and List,

we used in the past some markers on aircrafts to delineate distance on the ground during ungulates aerial surveys using fixed-width transect method (i.e. 500 m on each side of the aircraft). You are right when saying that permanent markers like metal parts will likely request some sort of certification delivered by the Transportation agency in each country. In Canada, these certifications are expensive and normally given for a specific aircraft (not a specific model but one certification for each aircraft used).  I do not know if you are trying to have a marker to delineate  only maximum width of your transect (like 500 m on each side), or if you are aiming at estimating distance of different objects from transect line.  I think the later will be difficult to achieve with good accuracy and you should maybe look for another method.

Serge

PS I suspect  that you are speaking French, so feel free to contact me off list  to continue the discussion in French if you want.

